{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YLeJg3comKmfKVaZtRT8sF","type":"tool_result","content":"0\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n1\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n2\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{url}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{Pairwise Type Confusability Predicts Schema-Induced Degradation\\\\in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction:\\\\Analysis and a Graph-Coloring Decomposition Framework}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{Anonymous}\n17\t\\date{}\n18\t\n19\t\\begin{document}\n20\t\\maketitle\n21\t\n22\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n23\t\\begin{abstract}\n24\tTraining-free LLM-based information extraction (IE) degrades severely on complex schemas, yet the mechanisms driving this degradation remain poorly understood.\n25\tWe conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating \\emph{type confusability} from \\emph{type count} as independent degradation factors across three IE tasks---named entity recognition (Few-NERD, 66 types), event detection (MAVEN, 168 types), and document-level relation extraction (DocRED, 96 types)---with two LLMs (Qwen3-32B and LLaMA-3.3-70B).\n26\tWe establish three findings.\n27\tFirst, pairwise type confusability, measured by embedding-based semantic similarity, independently predicts extraction quality degradation ($r{=}0.12$--$0.16$, $p{<}10^{-9}$), with high-confusability batches exhibiting $10$--$28\\times$ more type confusion errors.\n28\tSecond, count and confusability degrade performance through orthogonal channels: count causes entity omission (recall decline) while confusability causes type substitution errors, as confirmed by a $2{\\times}2$ factorial design and systematic error taxonomy.\n29\tThird, we propose \\textbf{Schema Decomposition via Graph Disentanglement (SDGD)}, a graph-coloring framework that partitions types into low-confusability batches."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RUPe8szH6qgkxttSJSecn8","session_id":"08912b4a-ab76-4663-b333-b897e4b7593f","uuid":"19e719cc-673b-49be-8553-16e7d5f83c0a","timestamp":"2026-05-15T12:27:43.316Z"}
