{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013P1MN7s8XNbF5Dbx1jLj6u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XdCtg4qtiYt2YBacnaLVE9","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T053106/best/paper.tex","offset":0,"limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5524,"cache_read_input_tokens":45755,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5524,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RUPe8szH6qgkxttSJSecn8","session_id":"08912b4a-ab76-4663-b333-b897e4b7593f","uuid":"2ad55575-c87a-4abf-b24f-7d22dc6e5475"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XdCtg4qtiYt2YBacnaLVE9","type":"tool_result","content":"0\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n1\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n2\t\\usepackage{times}\n3\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n5\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\n12\t\\title{Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction:\\\\Isolating Confusability from Count and Operationalizing SDGD}\n13\t\n14\t\\author{Anonymous}\n15\t\n16\t\\begin{document}\n17\t\\maketitle\n18\t\n19\t% ============================================================\n20\t\\begin{abstract}\n21\tTraining-free LLM information extraction degrades severely on complex schemas, yet the mechanisms driving this degradation remain poorly understood.\n22\tWe conduct the first controlled study isolating \\emph{type confusability} from \\emph{type count} in schema-conditioned LLM IE.\n23\tAcross two tasks (NER on Few-NERD with 66 types; Event Detection on MAVEN with 168 types) and two model families (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), we establish a \\textbf{multi-channel degradation model}: count drives recall decline through capacity bottleneck (Channel~1), while confusability drives type confusion errors through semantic interference (Channel~2).\n24\tBatch confusability score (BCS) independently predicts F1 degradation after controlling for type frequency (partial $r{=}0.32$, $p{=}0.002$ on Few-NERD; $r{=}0.22$, $p{=}0.004$ on MAVEN), with high-confusability batches exhibiting 10--28$\\times$ more type substitution errors.\n25\tWe operationalize these findings into \\textbf{Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition (SDGD)}, a graph-coloring framework that partitions types into low-confusability batches.\n26\tSDGD improves F1 by 7.6 points on Few-NERD and 6.0 points on MAVEN over single-pass extraction, matching per-type splitting quality at 22\\% of the latency cost.\n27\t\\end{abstract}\n28\t\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t\\section{Introduction}\n31\t\\label{sec:intro}\n32\t\n33\tLarge language models have become practical tools for training-free information extraction, enabling deployment in domains where annotated data is scarce or nonexistent---clinical records~\\cite{}, regulatory filings, and multilingual settings covering 50+ languages with no IE corpora.\n34\tHowever, performance degrades sharply as schema complexity increases: a 20--60 F1 gap separates training-free LLM IE from supervised systems on schemas with dozens of fine-grained types.\n35\t\n36\tExisting decomposition strategies address this gap with ad-hoc heuristics.\n37\tPer-type splitting~\\cite{} processes each type independently, eliminating inter-type confusion but losing cross-type context and incurring linear API cost.\n38\tSimilarity-based partitioning~\\cite{} uses type embeddings as implicit confusability proxies without empirical validation.\n39\tFixed pipeline decomposition~\\cite{} operates at the task level, ignoring intra-layer schema structure.\n40\tNone of these approaches isolates \\emph{what} makes schemas complex for LLMs.\n41\t\n42\tWe hypothesize that schema-induced degradation is a \\emph{multi-channel} process.\n43\t\\textbf{Channel~1} (Count $\\to$ Recall): more types in the prompt dilute attention, causing entity omission.\n44\t\\textbf{Channel~2} (Confusability $\\to$ Type Confusion): semantically similar co-present types interfere during type assignment, causing correct spans to receive wrong labels.\n45\tThese channels are orthogonal: reducing batch size addresses Channel~1; separating confusable types addresses Channel~2; the gains are additive.\n46\t\n47\tWe validate this model through three layers of evidence:\n48\t\\begin{enumerate}\n49\t    \\item \\textbf{Pairwise BCS-confusion correlation} (immune to type frequency confounds) confirms that embedding-based confusability predicts empirical type confusion ($r{=}0.16$--$0.19$, $p{<}10^{-9}$) across 2 datasets and 2 models."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RUPe8szH6qgkxttSJSecn8","session_id":"08912b4a-ab76-4663-b333-b897e4b7593f","uuid":"790b5e50-2100-4822-a842-956ad0f38460","timestamp":"2026-05-15T12:27:49.969Z"}
