{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Na3DZia5oWVHVzYjaMsN5Z","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n6\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n9\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n10\t\n11\t\\hypersetup{colorlinks=true,linkcolor=blue!60!black,citecolor=blue!60!black,urlcolor=blue!60!black}\n12\t\n13\t\\newcommand{\\ie}{i.e.}\n14\t\\newcommand{\\eg}{e.g.}\n15\t\\newcommand{\\cf}{cf.}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\etal}{et~al.}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\fone}{F\\textsubscript{1}}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Is Schema Complexity One-Dimensional?\\\\A Controlled Empirical Study of Type Count, Confusability,\\\\and Their Interaction in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous}\n22\t\\date{}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t% ============================================================\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t\n31\tTraining-free LLM information extraction degrades severely on complex schemas, yet what makes a schema ``complex'' remains poorly understood.\n32\tWe present the first controlled empirical study that disentangles two dimensions of schema complexity---\\emph{type count} and \\emph{type confusability}---across three IE tasks (NER, event detection, relation extraction), two LLM families (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), and two embedding spaces (Qwen3, MiniLM).\n33\tOur findings reveal that schema complexity is not one-dimensional.\n34\t(1)~Pairwise type confusability significantly predicts type confusion errors on certain model$\\times$embedding$\\times$task combinations ($r{=}0.156$, $p{<}10^{-11}$ for Qwen3 on Few-NERD; $r{=}0.115$, $p{<}10^{-42}$ for MiniLM on MAVEN), but the signal is highly model-dependent---LLaMA-3.3-70B shows no significant effect ($r{=}0.042$, $p{=}0.068$).\n35\t(2)~Count and confusability degrade performance through distinguishable error channels: count drives entity omission (miss rate $0.28{\\to}0.55$), while confusability drives type substitution ($4.8{\\times}$ higher in high-confusability batches).\n36\t(3)~Per-type confusability aggregation introduces a centrality confound in semantically homogeneous schemas, where pairwise analysis remains valid.\n37\t(4)~Counter-intuitively, confusability-minimizing schema decomposition \\emph{harms} performance ($\\Delta{=}{-}3.3$ to ${-}7.3$ F\\textsubscript{1} vs.\\ single-pass), because type co-presence provides disambiguation context that outweighs confusion costs.\n38\tThese findings establish that confusability effects are real but not universal, and that the intuitive ``separate confusable types'' strategy fails for LLMs that leverage comparative type reasoning.\n39\t\n40\t\\end{abstract}\n41\t\n42\t% ============================================================\n43\t\\section{Introduction}\n44\t\\label{sec:intro}\n45\t% ============================================================\n46\t\n47\tTraining-free LLM information extraction---using instruction-tuned LLMs with in-context type definitions but no task-specific training---has emerged as a practical paradigm for rapid deployment.\n48\tHowever, performance degrades sharply as schema complexity increases: schemas with $50{+}$ types can exhibit $20{-}60$ F\\textsubscript{1} gaps compared to supervised baselines.\n49\t\n50\tPrior work has addressed this degradation through schema decomposition: splitting large schemas into smaller batches for independent extraction."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F4mKoAGQenSYMfBcSrr8sy","session_id":"08912b4a-ab76-4663-b333-b897e4b7593f","uuid":"282bd564-e11a-4f77-b612-4184542fddb7","timestamp":"2026-05-15T12:28:08.242Z"}
