{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RUPe8szH6qgkxttSJSecn8","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n**Key findings from the review:**\n\n1. **Novelty is overstated.** The \"first controlled study\" claim needs qualification -- IEPile (ACL 2024) already uses semantically proximate types as hard negatives (same insight, different application), CROSSAGENTIE (ACL 2025) treats type confusion as a known problem, and the broader label confusion literature has established the similarity-confusion link. The novelty is in the *controlled factorial isolation*, not the finding itself.\n\n2. **Effect sizes are problematically small.** r=0.12--0.16 explains only 1--2.5% of variance. The paper does not address what drives the other 97--99%.\n\n3. **SDGD results may be unverified.** Decision D014 in the project log records SDGD-zero performing *worse* than baseline (F1=0.251 vs. 0.445). The paper reports +5.5pp gains, which appear to depend on a v2 redesign marked \"awaiting GPU verification.\" DocRED results also lack corresponding experiment artifacts.\n\n4. **The centrality confound is the strongest contribution** -- a genuine methodological finding with implications beyond this paper.\n\n5. **Related work is underdeveloped.** The paper's own literature map identifies 25+ relevant papers (PARSE, DEE, SPT, DLISC, GLiNER, KGGen, etc.) but the draft cites only 6.\n\n6. **Statistical concerns** include non-independent type pairs treated as independent observations, uncorrected multiple comparisons, and a \"marginally significant\" interaction (p=0.087).\n\nThe review is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T113743/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5951968592509811 (use SendMessage with to: 'a5951968592509811' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 58344\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 155983</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"08912b4a-ab76-4663-b333-b897e4b7593f","uuid":"c24c1115-d940-48f9-ba25-06393490bcce","timestamp":"2026-05-15T12:29:50.501Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, related work, and significance**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Pairwise Type Confusability Independently Predicts Extraction Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper studies why training-free LLM IE degrades on complex schemas. It isolates \"type confusability\" (embedding cosine similarity between type pairs) from \"type count\" as independent degradation channels across NER (Few-NERD, 66 types), RE (FewRel, 80 types; DocRED, 96 types), and ED (MAVEN, 168 types), using Qwen3-32B and LLaMA-3.3-70B.\n\nThree contributions:\n1. Pairwise confusability as cross-task signal: r=0.08–0.16, p&lt;1e-4 across tasks/models\n2. Multi-channel degradation model: count→omission (recall↓), confusability→substitution (precision↓), additive (interaction p=0.24), with error taxonomy showing 62% substitution in high-BCS vs 28% in low-BCS\n3. Centrality confound discovery: per-type aggregation reverses signal in homogeneous schemas (MAVEN per-type ρ=+0.19/+0.21 positive, opposite to Few-NERD's −0.293), plus 2×3 embedding ablation guidance\n\nAdditionally: SDGD framework (graph-coloring decomposition) reduces type confusion errors 24–35% but F1 gains NOT statistically significant (SDGD-zero +1.1pp p=0.18; SDGD-cal +2.6pp p=0.09).\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper (§2)\n- **Training-free LLM IE**: GPT-NER (Wang et al. 2023), Li et al. 2023, Wei et al. 2023, Ma et al. 2023 — establish paradigm, report degradation. UniNER (Zhou et al. 2024) per-type decomposition. GoLLIE (Sainz et al. 2024) code-style schema.\n- **Schema decomposition**: LLM-PEE (Zhang et al. 2024) groups by semantic similarity without validation.\n- **Context effects**: \"Lost in the Middle\" (Liu et al. 2024) — positional attention degradation, drawn as analogy.\n\n## Key Claims to Assess for Novelty/Significance\n1. \"First controlled study isolating type confusability from type count\"\n2. \"Multi-channel degradation model\" with error taxonomy\n3. \"Centrality confound\" as methodological discovery\n4. SDGD as \"confusability-minimizing schema decomposition\"\n5. The \"analysis is the contribution\" framing — paper is 80% analysis, 20% method\n\n## Key Numbers for Significance Assessment\n- Effect sizes are small: r=0.08–0.16 (pairwise), explaining ~1–2.5% of variance\n- SDGD F1 improvements not significant on any dataset\n- SDGD-zero vs random batching: only +0.7pp difference\n- The paper explicitly frames SDGD as \"proof-of-concept\"\n- Error taxonomy shows clear pattern (62% vs 28% substitution shift)\n- Centrality confound is well-analyzed with clear mechanism\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty/significance/positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty/significance/positioning)\n4. **Questions to Authors** (specific, numbered)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Preliminary Score** (1-6) with justification\n7. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Pay attention to:\n- Is the \"first controlled study\" claim justified? Are there prior works that do similar isolation?\n- Is the novelty primarily in the analysis (observational findings) or the method (SDGD)? If analysis, is the analysis deep/surprising enough for a top venue?\n- How does this compare to established work on label confusion in NLP (e.g., label confusion in classification, noisy labels literature)?\n- Is the \"Lost in the Middle\" analogy apt or superficial?\n- Does the related work section miss important prior art? (e.g., confusion-aware training, label similarity in few-shot learning, type-aware IE methods)\n- Is the centrality confound finding genuinely novel or a known statistical artifact (Simpson's paradox variant)?\n- The SDGD method is simple (graph coloring on cosine similarity graph) — is this enough methodological novelty for a top venue?\n- Significance: who benefits from these findings? Are the practitioner implications actionable enough?\n- Is the 80:20 analysis:method split appropriate for the venue?\n- Are the effect sizes (r=0.08–0.16) practically meaningful, or just statistically significant due to large n?\n","agentId":"a5951968592509811","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n**Key findings from the review:**\n\n1. **Novelty is overstated.** The \"first controlled study\" claim needs qualification -- IEPile (ACL 2024) already uses semantically proximate types as hard negatives (same insight, different application), CROSSAGENTIE (ACL 2025) treats type confusion as a known problem, and the broader label confusion literature has established the similarity-confusion link. The novelty is in the *controlled factorial isolation*, not the finding itself.\n\n2. **Effect sizes are problematically small.** r=0.12--0.16 explains only 1--2.5% of variance. The paper does not address what drives the other 97--99%.\n\n3. **SDGD results may be unverified.** Decision D014 in the project log records SDGD-zero performing *worse* than baseline (F1=0.251 vs. 0.445). The paper reports +5.5pp gains, which appear to depend on a v2 redesign marked \"awaiting GPU verification.\" DocRED results also lack corresponding experiment artifacts.\n\n4. **The centrality confound is the strongest contribution** -- a genuine methodological finding with implications beyond this paper.\n\n5. **Related work is underdeveloped.** The paper's own literature map identifies 25+ relevant papers (PARSE, DEE, SPT, DLISC, GLiNER, KGGen, etc.) but the draft cites only 6.\n\n6. **Statistical concerns** include non-independent type pairs treated as independent observations, uncorrected multiple comparisons, and a \"marginally significant\" interaction (p=0.087).\n\nThe review is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T113743/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":155983,"totalTokens":58344,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3984,"cache_read_input_tokens":53893,"output_tokens":466,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3984},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":466,"cache_read_input_tokens":53893,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3984,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3984,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":3,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
